course: dictionary
lesson: 6196
title: Misleading words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To mislead someone is to make them believe something that is not true. You can do this with wrong facts, or by being unclear on purpose. Mislead is a verb, so it can change: misleads, misled, misleading.
explain: «Watch out for half-lies: vague words meant to mislead ... are not honest, even if every word sounds clever.» Here the words could all be true, yet they still trick you. So a sentence can avoid a lie and still mislead.
explain: «Saying "I'm about to" just to get someone off your back misleads them.» The speaker is not saying anything false about the words themselves. But the listener is led to believe something wrong about the plans. That is why you should ask: does this give the whole picture?
fact: To mislead means to make someone believe something that is not true.
fact: Mislead can also mean to lead someone in the wrong direction.
fact: Misleading describes something that gives the wrong idea, such as misleading information or a misleading graph.
fact: A lie is not the only way to mislead: leaving out important parts can mislead too.
fact: Mislead is a verb: mislead, misleads, misled, misleading.
exercise: «Leaving out important parts can mislead too.» What does mislead mean here? | *Make someone believe something untrue by what is left out | Tell a story about a lost dog | Count the words in a sentence | Make someone laugh loudly
exercise: A graph uses a strange scale, so small numbers look huge. What can this do to the reader? | It helps the reader count faster | It shows the numbers exactly as they are | *It can mislead the reader into a wrong idea | It makes the graph easier to read in the dark
exercise: Lena says «I'm about to start» just to get Dad off her back. What does this do to Dad? | It makes him laugh | It proves she is honest | *It misleads him about what she is really doing | It helps him understand her plan
test: Which word is the past tense of mislead? | *Misled | Misleading | Misleads | Mislay
test: Which sentence uses misleading correctly? | The cake was misleading and very tasty | He misleading the dog to the park | The sign was clear and told us the right way | *The advert used misleading pictures to make the toy look bigger than it was
exam: Which is an example of misleading someone without telling a lie? | Answering a question with the full, honest truth | Saying thank you to a friend | Telling a joke about a cat | *Giving vague half-truths that leave out the important part
